00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.800
So let's start with
that introduction.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.287
So Cloud Storage, it's
actually object storage,

00:01:35.287 --> 00:01:36.370
hopefully all of you know.

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:41.380
So we also have block and
file products as part of GCP.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.870
But our Cloud Storage product
is our object storage offering,

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:48.730
which is really the native
cloud format for data.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:53.590
And what makes, in the end,
the key values of our solution

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.930
are that we're trusted,
that we're secure,

00:01:55.930 --> 00:01:57.070
we're smart, and simple.

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:01.270
And you'll see all of these
reflected in the capabilities

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.130
of the product.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:06.910
So why have a cloud
storage offering?

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:10.900
It all comes back to
the origins of Google

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.860
and the data centers that we've
established around the world

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.050
to deliver all of the
products and services

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.870
that you're familiar
with outside of GCP,

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.670
outside of the Cloud product.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.420
And we had to build extremely
fast, capable networks

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:26.710
across the globe.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:31.210
We had to create exabytes
of storage capacity

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:34.660
to handle all of the properties
like Gmail and YouTube

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:35.800
and Search.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.740
And so why not have
a cloud offering

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.250
that would allow customers
to directly use the Google

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.380
infrastructure that has
been built successfully

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.160
over all of these years?

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.700
So that's one of the
key differentiators

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:49.750
for Cloud Storage.

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:53.380
Even though we're a storage
platform, a storage product,

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.880
in the end, it's
the network that

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.930
is one of the biggest
advantages of Google Cloud

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.300
Platform, its hundreds
of thousands of miles

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:03.970
of fiber optic cable
across the ocean

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.710
as well as within continents.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:08.470
And we have more edge
and peering capabilities

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:12.210
than any other public cloud.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.350
So the three use cases
that I'll be covering today

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.950
are content storage
and delivery-- and so

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:21.900
this ranges from everything
from video and music streaming,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.500
website hosting,
multimedia offerings,

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.270
mobile app development.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.420
It's any use case
where you have content

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:33.320
and you're bringing it
out to a large user base,

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.770
possibly within a continent,
possibly globally.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:39.130
Compute analytics and
machine learning, of course,

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.900
is a very important area that's
receiving a lot of attention

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:42.860
these days.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:46.310
And here you see a lot of use
in manufacturing, genomics,

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:50.000
e-commerce, internet of
things, all brings customers

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.136
to Cloud Storage.

00:03:51.136 --> 00:03:53.510
And then finally, I'll cover
the backup and archiving use

00:03:53.510 --> 00:03:56.900
case, which is of particular
interest for our Nearline

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.760
and Coldline products, so
long-term archives of content

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.120
where it's not being accessed
frequently, but when you do

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.770
need it, you want
it to be there.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:11.330
So some of the most
important features--

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:12.780
it's a unified experience.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.900
So I'll be talking about
different ways of storing

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.550
your data in Cloud Storage.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:21.390
But no matter how you store
it, it's the same API,

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.590
regardless of storage class.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.390
It's always strongly consistent.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.240
So when you store
data and then you

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.230
do a bucket listing of that
data a short time after,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.210
you're guaranteed to see the
data that you just wrote.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.870
And that's not always the case
with cloud storage offerings.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:39.570
And that simplifies
application development.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.960
We offer a wide array of
geo-redundancy offerings.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.640
So not only just picking--
you can, of course,

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:47.820
just pick one region and
we'll store your data

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.090
in that one region.

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:52.080
But if you want to actually
have automatic replication

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.600
and have your data served
from multiple regions

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.090
to isolate you against
the very rare possibility

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:00.240
of a region-wide outage,
we offer a variety

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:01.930
of ways of making that happen.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:04.590
And then finally, you saw
the network that I just

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:06.110
showed you for Google.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:08.340
Well, that allows us,
along with the data

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.340
centers that we've put in place,
to have your data be scalable

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.360
up to exabytes of content.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.690
So in our case, petabytes of
content is not a scary number.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.760
Hundreds of petabytes,
even, is something

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:22.527
that can be very
easily accomplished.

00:05:22.527 --> 00:05:24.610
Exabytes, we may want to
plan a little bit for it.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:29.880
But we have a lot of
multi-exabyte data center

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.850
installations, as well.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.300
So what are the key
terms for object storage?

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:37.250
So first of all,
what's an object?

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:38.720
That's the most base piece.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:40.700
That's actually,
typically, a file

00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:44.090
that you've written as an
object to a particular location.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:47.720
And this has an object
name associated with it.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:49.040
Where do you store objects?

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:49.940
Inside buckets.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:52.100
So buckets are the container.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:53.540
All of the objects go inside.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.520
You have bucket name.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.290
Where do buckets go?

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.550
Well, that's locations.

00:05:58.550 --> 00:06:02.360
So a location for Cloud
Storage is you either pick

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:04.460
a region or a
multi-region, which

00:06:04.460 --> 00:06:05.750
encompasses multiple regions.

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:07.520
I'll talk about that in a sec.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.690
And that location is where the
bucket is associated with it.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:14.450
And all of the contents for
that bucket, all of the objects,

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:17.520
end up being stored
within that location.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.340
And then finally we have the
concept of a storage class.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:23.300
And a storage class is
nothing more, nothing less

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.030
than determining the
access frequencies

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:30.640
that you expect your
objects to be served at.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:33.080
It's the availability
levels and how important

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:34.700
the availability is.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.530
And it also sets
the pricing profile.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.634
So an object that has to
be served a lot and in more

00:06:40.634 --> 00:06:42.800
of a hot tier, of course,
is charged for at a higher

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.070
level than archive data.

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:50.240
So there are two types of
locations for Cloud Storage.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.580
One is the
multi-regional offering

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.680
and the other is
regional in nature.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.110
And your regional
offering, which

00:06:57.110 --> 00:07:01.820
is very similar to what is
available with other competing

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:04.490
cloud offerings, you basically
you pick a region anywhere

00:07:04.490 --> 00:07:07.700
in the world, and your data
is stored within that region.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:10.760
Multi-regional is interesting
because we automatically

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:14.030
offer geo-diversity as part
of the storage offering.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.850
So we'll replicate data
across more than one

00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:18.550
region within that multi-region.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:20.960
And a multi-region is
usually a continent.

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.370
So we have a US
multi-region, for example,

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:26.509
that constitutes all
of the large data

00:07:26.509 --> 00:07:28.550
centers that Google has
within the United States.

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:33.050
Similarly, we have one for
the European Union, called EU.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:36.150
And we have one
for Asia, as well.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:40.070
So that allows you to pick
a continent, basically.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:43.440
Just create a bucket
in that continent.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.860
And we will automatically
store the data in more than one

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:47.840
location within that continent.

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:50.840
And so even if there is a
hurricane or some major event

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:53.930
that takes down a region,
as unlikely as that is,

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:55.700
your data will
still be immediately

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:58.430
available from the
other locations.

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:00.440
And that replication
is done without having

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:05.300
to set up a replication service
or manage it in any way.

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:08.710
It stays a single namespace.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:13.200
So I've talked about a little
bit of this already, but one

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.000
of the key distinctions
between making

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.670
that decision between
regional and multi-regional

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.700
comes down to trading
off performance

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:23.130
predictability and
high throughput

00:08:23.130 --> 00:08:25.210
for co-locating your data.

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:28.110
So if you are, for example,
running analytics workloads

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:31.680
and it's very important that
your GCE installation is

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.946
in the same region
as your data, that's

00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:35.820
something that you can
accomplish very easily

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.919
with regional storage.

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:40.590
With multi-regional,
as you'll see,

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.500
while it doesn't allow that
tight co-location of data

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:45.880
and does result in a
little bit higher latency,

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:49.710
it's perfect for content
serving and other areas where

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:51.870
the goal is to host
that data and make it

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:53.550
available across the internet.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:59.750
So I mentioned storage classes
a couple of slides ago.

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:01.870
And storage classes
basically determine

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:03.890
how the data is stored.

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:07.150
So part of it is whether it's
multi-regional or regional.

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:10.510
Those are basically our
storage classes for hot data.

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:12.790
So it has the highest
service level agreements

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:16.840
associated with them, also,
of course, the higher pricing

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:20.920
to reflect the higher SLAs
and the higher performance.

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.560
And then we also have Nearline
and Coldline offerings

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.020
that basically allow you
to tier your storage down

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:28.330
to lower-cost
offerings where you

00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:34.160
don't expect to be accessing
your data frequently.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:37.660
So this shows the data
placement at a very, very high

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:38.950
abstracted level.

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:41.200
So don't be distracted
by that some of them

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.870
have two boxes and
others have one.

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:46.600
In all cases, the
storage is there

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:48.350
with very high durability.

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:53.200
So we're talking about 11
nines worth of durability.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:56.470
What's shown here is actually
the availability model

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:57.700
across regions.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:01.750
So multi-regional storage
class means that the data

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:03.670
exists in more than one region.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:07.090
With regional and Nearline
and Coldline classes,

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:08.560
they're stored
within one region.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:11.380
But they're still stored
across availability zones,

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:14.230
allowing large sections
of the data center

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:17.110
or within a metro area to go
offline with the data still

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:18.370
being accessible.

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:21.310
So no matter how you store and
no matter which storage class

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:23.710
it is, the storage
is highly durable.

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:25.750
It's just a question of
that additional question

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:29.665
of the isolation against
potential regional outages.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:34.900
So that's the
brief introduction.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:37.690
Now I'm going to go ahead and
start with content storage

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:39.550
and delivery, which
is the first major use

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:42.990
case for Cloud Storage.

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:44.880
So there are actually
four common setups

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:46.980
that we see with
content serving,

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:49.560
and the simplest to
understand is direct serving.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:52.440
Basically, you put your
content in the Cloud.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:54.990
And you serve it
directly from the Cloud.

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:58.950
You pay egress rates based on
where the data is being read.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:00.540
And you take advantage
automatically

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:03.240
of all of the edge caching
and points of presence

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:06.480
that Google has everywhere.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:10.040
However, we also have
a Cloud CDN offering.

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:13.460
And what this does is it
allows you to actually save

00:11:13.460 --> 00:11:16.040
on the cost
associated with egress

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.140
if the data is already cached.

00:11:18.140 --> 00:11:22.040
It also allows you
to take advantage

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:26.210
of some additional
security offerings.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:28.680
I'll get into some more
details there in a moment.

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.310
There's also the ability
to do custom front ends

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:34.010
if you want to have additional
intelligence and more

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:35.580
dynamic content.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.490
And then finally,
you can also use

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:39.320
Google Cloud Storage
in conjunction

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:40.970
with independent CDNs.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:42.830
So these are all possibilities.

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:46.260
Let me go into some more details
on the next couple of slides.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:49.340
So direct serving, what
would the best practices

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:50.300
be for direct serving?

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.970
So one of them is to use
the multi-regional class.

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:55.850
This ensures that you
have high availability

00:11:55.850 --> 00:12:00.380
for all of your data
with that geo-redundancy.

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:02.960
And you can also set
cache control headers

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:05.240
to enable edge caching.

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:08.990
So let's go ahead and
take a look at a demo just

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:12.080
to see how you still
get some advantages

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:14.740
from our local caching.

00:12:17.542 --> 00:12:19.371
MICHAEL YU: Here we
have a sample bucket

00:12:19.371 --> 00:12:20.120
that I've created.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.180
It's called io107-demo.

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:24.899
And what I want to show
you is that this bucket was

00:12:24.899 --> 00:12:26.190
created in the Europe location.

00:12:26.190 --> 00:12:30.670
It's a multi-regional bucket,
so it's pretty far away from us.

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:35.400
And I've created an object
in here called google.png.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:37.740
And we can go ahead
and open that up.

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:41.070
And it's the Google logo,
so nothing too special

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:43.200
here so far.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:45.600
I'm going go ahead and open
up the developer tools here.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.060
And this will give us a little
bit of timing information

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:52.300
for loading this PNG.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:12:54.010
And you can see that
when I reloaded it,

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:56.960
it took about 366 milliseconds.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.800
And that makes sense
because this asset is

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.840
hosted all the way in Europe.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:04.450
So first of all, one
thing I wanted to show

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:06.940
is that even though this
object is in Europe,

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:09.670
it's actually using
Google's global network.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:11.410
And it's connecting right away.

00:13:11.410 --> 00:13:13.300
And most of the
request is actually

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:15.100
transited on Google's network.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:16.780
And one way we can
take a look at that

00:13:16.780 --> 00:13:19.300
is by running a trace
route to the domain here,

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:22.279
storage.googleapis.com.

00:13:22.279 --> 00:13:23.570
So let me go ahead and do that.

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:34.660
And so this is tracing
the path to the server.

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:37.840
And you can see here at the
very end, one of the things

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:39.010
is the latency.

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:41.240
It's about five or
six milliseconds.

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:44.620
So this is clearly somewhere
closer than Europe.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:48.010
And we can actually take
a little bit closer look

00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:49.060
to see where that is.

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:50.220
So if you look at
the server name,

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:51.845
it's actually a little
bit interesting.

00:13:51.845 --> 00:13:53.680
It says NUQ and then
a few other things.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:55.960
It also says 1e100.net.

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:58.100
So what does this all mean?

00:13:58.100 --> 00:14:00.670
Well, first of all,
1e100.net, if you search

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:04.300
for the definition of googol,
which is actually what

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:06.910
our company should have been
named, but we misspelled it,

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:08.560
it's 1e100.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:13.750
So this is kind of like a code
name for a device that we own.

00:14:13.750 --> 00:14:15.240
And what about NUQ?

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:20.090
NUQ ends up being
Moffett Federal Air

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:22.140
Field, which is an airfield
pretty close by here

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:23.694
in San Jose.

00:14:23.694 --> 00:14:25.860
The devices aren't actually
located in the airfield.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:27.330
But it gives you
a hint that we're

00:14:27.330 --> 00:14:29.820
ending up terminating the
connection somewhere in the Bay

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:30.547
Area.

00:14:30.547 --> 00:14:32.130
And once we terminate
that connection,

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:35.730
we then go ahead and transit the
request across our own network

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:38.300
all the way to Europe.

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:41.820
OK, the request is mostly
transiting over our network.

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.920
But it is taking a long time.

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.230
So in order to
enable edge caching,

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:48.780
its just a simple matter of
changing the cache control

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:49.397
headers.

00:14:49.397 --> 00:14:51.480
So here I want to change
the catch control headers

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:52.515
from no cache to public.

00:14:58.390 --> 00:15:04.160
And now if I go ahead
and reload a few times,

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:06.830
you can see that it's
gone down from about 300,

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.870
400 milliseconds down to
about 15 milliseconds.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:11.510
And that's because
this asset is now

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:13.850
being served directly
from the edge cache

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:14.900
here in the Bay Area.

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:15.622
So--

00:15:15.622 --> 00:15:16.830
GEOFFREY NOER: OK, thank you.

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:18.820
Very good.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:21.525
And now let's change
back to the content.

00:15:27.351 --> 00:15:27.850
Great.

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:30.142
So now we've seen the
edge cache in action.

00:15:30.142 --> 00:15:31.600
So I think that
hopefully gives you

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:35.590
a little bit of a clue as to
why content delivery is such

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:39.410
a good fit for Cloud Storage.

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:41.730
And so what about Cloud CDN?

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:43.810
You automatically
get some advantage

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:46.210
from that, that you
just saw with the demo.

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:48.940
But Cloud CDN is an
additional offering

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:53.300
that allows you to
control the custom domain.

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:55.720
So if you want
HTTPS, if you want

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:57.790
to take advantage of some
differentiated pricing

00:15:57.790 --> 00:16:00.880
by not being charged
egress, lower egress

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:04.270
pricing when you're correctly
hitting the edge caches,

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:08.260
and it also allows you to handle
static and dynamic request

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:09.510
handling from the same domain.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:12.930
So some valuable
additional features.

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.410
You can also do
custom front ends.

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:17.680
And this can be done with
or without Cloud CDN.

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:19.420
But basically this
allows you to also

00:16:19.420 --> 00:16:24.910
have a GCE instance doing
some custom dynamic transforms

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:29.620
of data, authorizations, that
sort of thing, in the path.

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:30.790
And then the data is served.

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:32.470
And again, it can be cached.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:36.130
So we see a lot of either direct
serving or the use of Cloud CDN

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:38.260
and/or custom front ends.

00:16:38.260 --> 00:16:41.560
It all depends on the nature
of the content delivery

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:44.040
requirements.

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:47.220
So the final aspect here
is using independent CDNs.

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:51.590
So yes, Google provides a
lot of offerings that allow

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:53.510
you to do it all from Google.

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:56.930
But there are actually a lot of
valid and very useful reasons

00:16:56.930 --> 00:16:59.060
why you might want
to consider using

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:01.830
an independent CDN as part
of the picture, as well.

00:17:01.830 --> 00:17:05.990
So we have partnerships with
Akamai, Cloudflare, Fastly,

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:09.060
Highwinds, Level 3, Limelight,
Verizon, and others.

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:13.040
And all of them offer specific
features, reach, or redundancy

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:14.270
for their particular network.

00:17:14.270 --> 00:17:16.760
They may be specialized
for a particular industry.

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:18.950
They may have
offerings and features

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:22.560
that you want to take advantage
of that we don't have yet.

00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:24.650
And really, the
thing that you need

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:27.430
to consider when you're
using an independent CDN

00:17:27.430 --> 00:17:30.020
is basically the concept
of an origin shield

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:33.200
and where that's
located geographically.

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:36.170
So typically with
an independent CDN,

00:17:36.170 --> 00:17:40.250
instead of using a
multi-regional storage class,

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:42.900
you should actually use
a regional storage class.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:44.590
And the reason for
that is you can

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:47.240
co-locate it close to
where the origin shield is

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:48.840
for the independent CDN.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.650
That origin shield is
basically the block

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:55.220
between ensuring that not
too much egress happens

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:57.140
from Google.

00:17:57.140 --> 00:18:00.890
So it helps control your
costs and keep the caching

00:18:00.890 --> 00:18:02.960
within your independent CDN.

00:18:05.890 --> 00:18:08.250
OK, so with that, that's
a brief introduction

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.200
into the content
delivery direction

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:11.750
with the Cloud Storage.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:13.500
And now I'd like to
bring Michael back up.

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:15.930
And he will talk a little
bit about compute analytics

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:17.140
and machine learning.

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:19.390
MICHAEL YU: Thank you, Geoff.

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:19.990
OK.

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:21.970
So I'm going to
cover essentially

00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:23.530
data processing use cases.

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:25.570
And there's quite a
lot of variety here.

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:28.780
So quite a lot of variety,
quite a lot of specialized tools

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:30.700
and frameworks and
services you can use.

00:18:30.700 --> 00:18:33.033
So we're not going to have
enough time to go into those.

00:18:33.033 --> 00:18:35.740
So we'll, instead, cover a
few generic best practices

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:39.810
and rules of thumb that
should apply to most of them.

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:42.590
So let's first take a
look at how GCS or Cloud

00:18:42.590 --> 00:18:46.370
Storage is used within these
data processing pipelines.

00:18:46.370 --> 00:18:48.050
And the first case
is source data.

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:50.652
This is the primary
input into your pipeline.

00:18:50.652 --> 00:18:52.610
Cloud Storage is pretty
popular because there's

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.410
a variety of
different data formats

00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:55.850
and object storage
doesn't really

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:58.670
care what your format is.

00:18:58.670 --> 00:19:00.530
For most pipelines,
you end up reading

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:01.650
the majority of your data.

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:03.870
So you really care about
aggregate throughput.

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:06.200
So the key thing here
is to use regional

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:09.004
and to co-locate your
storage with your compute.

00:19:09.004 --> 00:19:11.420
That's going to give you the
highest aggregate throughput.

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:13.460
It's going to keep
all of the transfer

00:19:13.460 --> 00:19:17.280
within the in-region network.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:18.850
The second type is
intermediate data.

00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:21.266
This is data that's produced
by one stage of your pipeline

00:19:21.266 --> 00:19:23.550
and then consumed by another
stage of your pipeline.

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:25.049
And here it's a
little bit trickier.

00:19:25.049 --> 00:19:27.330
If you care about
data persistence,

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:30.450
for example, if your pipeline
is doing something expensive

00:19:30.450 --> 00:19:33.180
and you want to be able to
resume the computation midway

00:19:33.180 --> 00:19:36.750
through if something goes wrong,
then GCS makes sense to use.

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:39.570
And in that case, the
recommendation is the same.

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:42.030
Go ahead and use
regional and co-locate

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:44.492
your storage with your data.

00:19:44.492 --> 00:19:46.200
However, in some cases,
it actually makes

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:48.270
sense to not use GCS
and to use something

00:19:48.270 --> 00:19:49.860
a little bit more
transient, where

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:52.140
you don't care about persisting
this intermediate data

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.100
and you can get a little
bit higher throughput.

00:19:54.100 --> 00:19:56.280
So for example, in
some Hadoop cases,

00:19:56.280 --> 00:20:01.860
you can use HDFS and run HDFS
on top of a persistent disk

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:03.120
or local SSD.

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:05.800
And that will enable you
to scale even higher.

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:08.400
We also have things like
Cloud Dataflow, which

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:11.580
offer more specialized
services like a shuffle service

00:20:11.580 --> 00:20:12.750
for specific operations.

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:14.730
And so when you can use
those, those sometimes

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:17.850
make sense rather
than running your own

00:20:17.850 --> 00:20:21.800
and using GCS as
intermediate data.

00:20:21.800 --> 00:20:25.030
Third type is side
inputs and staging data.

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:27.750
So unlike primary
inputs, primary inputs

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:29.970
you typically divide
your input into shards

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:32.670
and you distribute them
to various compute nodes.

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:35.010
Side inputs are inputs
that you distribute

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:36.310
to all of your compute nodes.

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:39.660
So this might be, for
example, a mapping table

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:42.540
which each compute node can then
transform your primary inputs

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:43.680
with.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.420
Similarly, staging
data is any data

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:48.810
that you would stage on each of
your compute nodes beforehand.

00:20:48.810 --> 00:20:51.120
So here you can go
ahead and use two GCS.

00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:52.050
You can use regional.

00:20:52.050 --> 00:20:53.841
But it's actually OK
to use multi-regional,

00:20:53.841 --> 00:20:55.770
as well, if you want
higher availability.

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:57.570
And the reason why is
that because you're

00:20:57.570 --> 00:21:01.140
doing repeated reads,
GCS will automatically

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:02.860
cache your data locally.

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:04.380
And so those
repeated fetches will

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:05.880
come from within the region.

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:07.838
And you're not going to
be constrained in terms

00:21:07.838 --> 00:21:10.610
of aggregate throughput.

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:12.190
And then finally,
final outputs--

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:14.150
this one's a little
bit trickier.

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.880
The storage class
recommendation really

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:18.160
depends on what you're going
to do with those outputs.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:21.010
So if you're going to use these
outputs to do content serving,

00:21:21.010 --> 00:21:23.380
you might use the
recommendations that Geoff just

00:21:23.380 --> 00:21:24.880
gave for content serving.

00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:26.820
If you're going to use them
to feed into another pipeline,

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:28.403
than you might use
the recommendations

00:21:28.403 --> 00:21:30.010
we gave earlier for source data.

00:21:30.010 --> 00:21:33.147
So it really depends
on your use case

00:21:33.147 --> 00:21:35.480
to determine what kind of
storage class you want to use.

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:40.300
So next, one common
question we get

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:42.910
is how large should
my objects be?

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:45.877
I have a large object, should
I fetch that in chunks?

00:21:45.877 --> 00:21:48.460
If I want to upload an object,
should I upload that in chunks?

00:21:48.460 --> 00:21:50.110
And to understand the
recommendation here,

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:52.240
we actually need to take
a look at the performance

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:54.011
profile of Cloud Storage.

00:21:54.011 --> 00:21:55.760
These are very, very
rough rules of thumb.

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:58.401
I've rounded them up to make
them a little bit easier

00:21:58.401 --> 00:21:58.900
to remember.

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:01.630
They're powers of
two as well, roughly.

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:04.570
And here we see that we have
about a 100 millisecond time

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:06.070
to first byte on reads.

00:22:06.070 --> 00:22:10.030
We have about a 200 millisecond
fix latency cost for writes.

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:12.130
Now, as the object
sizes get larger,

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:15.610
you're able to achieve about
800 megabits per second on read

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:17.411
and 400 megabits per
second on writes.

00:22:17.411 --> 00:22:18.910
So the key thing I
want to call here

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:21.760
is that there is a
relatively high fixed latency

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:23.145
cost per request.

00:22:23.145 --> 00:22:24.520
And so that means
if you're going

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:25.990
to be doing a lot
of small requests

00:22:25.990 --> 00:22:27.640
and you're doing
them serially, you're

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:29.139
paying that latency every time.

00:22:29.139 --> 00:22:31.430
And your aggregate throughput
is not going to be great.

00:22:31.430 --> 00:22:34.900
And that's what this graph on
the right hand side is showing.

00:22:34.900 --> 00:22:36.670
The smaller the
request size, the lower

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:38.211
the aggregate
throughput you're going

00:22:38.211 --> 00:22:41.880
to get when you're issuing
these requests sequentially.

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:45.540
So the general best practice is
to favor larger request sizes

00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:47.580
and object sizes
whenever you can.

00:22:47.580 --> 00:22:50.280
We recommend typically
two megabytes or larger,

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:52.380
if possible.

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:55.350
If you do need to do a
smaller request size,

00:22:55.350 --> 00:22:57.540
go ahead and parallelize
those small requests.

00:22:57.540 --> 00:22:59.760
So for example, one of
the common things we see

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:02.455
are people move an existing
pipeline over to the Cloud.

00:23:02.455 --> 00:23:04.080
And they might have
something like they

00:23:04.080 --> 00:23:06.570
might list files and then
perform some operation

00:23:06.570 --> 00:23:08.700
on each file one by one.

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:10.350
If you, instead,
parallelize those,

00:23:10.350 --> 00:23:13.116
you're going to be able to get
a lot better overall throughput.

00:23:17.102 --> 00:23:18.560
So next I want to
talk a little bit

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:21.800
about autoscaling for Cloud
Storage and some of the best

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:22.640
practices there.

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:24.265
And to understand
that, we have to look

00:23:24.265 --> 00:23:27.980
at how Cloud Storage ends
up sharding or splitting up

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:28.970
your data.

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:33.130
So Cloud Storage uses something
called range-based sharding.

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:34.730
What that means is
it takes your data

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:37.494
and it splits into
lexicographical ranges.

00:23:37.494 --> 00:23:38.910
And so in this
particular example,

00:23:38.910 --> 00:23:41.780
we have three ranges, A
through G, G through S, and S

00:23:41.780 --> 00:23:43.460
through the end of the alphabet.

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:47.960
And any object whose name
falls within the range

00:23:47.960 --> 00:23:50.589
will get assigned to that
corresponding shared.

00:23:50.589 --> 00:23:52.880
So for example, if I have an
object that starts with B,

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:56.510
it will fall into that
first shard, A through G.

00:23:56.510 --> 00:24:00.680
And the reason why we do this
type of sharding strategy

00:24:00.680 --> 00:24:02.930
is to provide
consistent listing.

00:24:02.930 --> 00:24:05.040
So imagine I want to
list all the objects that

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:09.620
start with T. This is actually
pretty easy to service.

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:12.560
It simply needs to
query a single shard.

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:15.080
And that shard has all
of the information needed

00:24:15.080 --> 00:24:18.410
to respond to that query.

00:24:18.410 --> 00:24:21.890
So in contrast, if you look
at the hash-based sharding

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:25.670
approach, this approach you
essentially, for example,

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:27.920
you would hash the
name of the object.

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:30.140
You might mod it
by four and then

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:34.250
assign objects to shards
based off of that mod.

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:37.580
And if we were to service that
same list objects request,

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:39.710
the problem is we
now no longer know

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:42.890
which shards contain
objects that start with T.

00:24:42.890 --> 00:24:46.770
So we have to query
every single shard.

00:24:46.770 --> 00:24:48.710
And what happens
if a shard is slow,

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:52.250
like that yellow arrow, or
a shard returns an error,

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:53.750
like the red arrow?

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:57.420
In that case, we
have a tough choice.

00:24:57.420 --> 00:25:01.460
We can either wait or ignore
the error, in which case

00:25:01.460 --> 00:25:04.310
we're returning an inconsistent
or incomplete listing--

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:05.000
sorry.

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:07.370
We could either ignore
the error, in which case

00:25:07.370 --> 00:25:10.220
we would be returning
a incomplete listing,

00:25:10.220 --> 00:25:12.560
or we could retry or serve
the error, in which case

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:15.410
we'd be providing a
higher error rate overall.

00:25:15.410 --> 00:25:17.750
And you can tell, as the
number of shards increases,

00:25:17.750 --> 00:25:19.850
this becomes a bigger
and bigger problem.

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:22.020
So this is why we use
range-based sharding.

00:25:25.100 --> 00:25:28.080
So GCS, like I mentioned,
it automatically scales.

00:25:28.080 --> 00:25:30.620
So it will detect when a
particular range is getting

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:34.060
hot, in this case, G through S.
And it will go ahead and split

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:37.754
that into two smaller ranges to
try to split the load evenly.

00:25:37.754 --> 00:25:39.170
And so this happens
automatically.

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:42.740
And most of the time, you
don't have to worry about it.

00:25:42.740 --> 00:25:46.670
Generally speaking, you can get
about 1,000 writes per second

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:50.180
and about 5,000 reads
per second without having

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:51.860
to worry about anything at all.

00:25:51.860 --> 00:25:55.190
Beyond that, it often takes
up to about 20 minutes for GCS

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:57.650
to figure out that a
shard needs to be split.

00:25:57.650 --> 00:26:00.060
And it will go ahead
and split that.

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:01.979
So for many use
cases, you really

00:26:01.979 --> 00:26:03.270
don't have to worry about this.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:06.690
However, if you are scaling up
beyond some of these numbers,

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:09.480
then one of the best
practices is really

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:11.730
to make sure you're
distributing your load evenly.

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:15.450
This really helps out our
auto-splitting algorithm

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:17.871
to scale load evenly.

00:26:17.871 --> 00:26:20.370
So I'm going to talk about this
in a little bit more detail.

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:23.670
And one of the most common ways
that load is not distributed

00:26:23.670 --> 00:26:26.039
evenly is sequential access.

00:26:26.039 --> 00:26:28.080
So I'm going to go through
a little bit of detail

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:29.820
to kind of give you
a flavor for why this

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:31.540
is so challenging to deal with.

00:26:31.540 --> 00:26:34.960
So I imagine we have
sequential access where

00:26:34.960 --> 00:26:38.340
we're accessing objects g, h,
i, j, k, et cetera, all the way

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:39.720
through q.

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:44.580
And imagine we had
split our shards like I

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:46.030
showed in the previous slide.

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:47.830
So we have a shard
of G through N,

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:52.010
and another shard, N
through R. The problem here

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:54.740
is that when you do sequential
access, the first set

00:26:54.740 --> 00:26:59.660
of accesses all hit one
shard, the G through N shard.

00:26:59.660 --> 00:27:02.990
And then once you get to the
second half of the accesses,

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:06.580
they all hit the second
shard, the N through R shard.

00:27:06.580 --> 00:27:08.060
And so at any given
point in time,

00:27:08.060 --> 00:27:10.970
you're still lasering or
hot spotting a single shard.

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:12.650
And splitting those
shards hasn't really

00:27:12.650 --> 00:27:15.800
distributed that load across
the two shards evenly.

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:18.965
So this hasn't really
helped us scale.

00:27:23.140 --> 00:27:25.450
You might ask, well, how
can we get around this?

00:27:25.450 --> 00:27:27.241
And one of the ways
you can get around this

00:27:27.241 --> 00:27:29.320
is to further split
into more shards.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:31.090
So here's an example
where we split it

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:32.560
into very fine-grained shards.

00:27:32.560 --> 00:27:37.520
Each shard is only taking
a single letter at a time.

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:41.320
And so this definitely works
in terms of scaling load.

00:27:41.320 --> 00:27:43.780
But the problem is that
there's fragmentation.

00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:46.240
And fragmentation,
again, basically

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:50.740
boils down to how do you
provide consistent listing?

00:27:50.740 --> 00:27:55.390
So again, when we only had
two shards, issuing a listing

00:27:55.390 --> 00:27:58.630
request only needed to talk
to, say, one shard at a time,

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:01.245
versus now, with
many, many shards,

00:28:01.245 --> 00:28:02.620
the same listing
request is going

00:28:02.620 --> 00:28:04.930
to have to split up and
talk to multiple shards

00:28:04.930 --> 00:28:05.836
at the same time.

00:28:05.836 --> 00:28:07.210
And we run into
the same problems

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:10.090
where any one shard can be
slow or can return an error.

00:28:10.090 --> 00:28:11.680
And that will cause
a listing problem.

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:13.870
So this is essentially
a fundamental trade-off

00:28:13.870 --> 00:28:19.620
between strong listing
consistency and hot-spotting.

00:28:19.620 --> 00:28:23.850
So here are a couple of ways
you can work around this.

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:26.670
If you're doing bulk operations
and they're doing them

00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:29.550
sequentially, you can try
to do them in parallel

00:28:29.550 --> 00:28:31.170
under different prefixes.

00:28:31.170 --> 00:28:33.840
And this will cause, in
this particular case,

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:36.090
foo and bar are
different prefixes.

00:28:36.090 --> 00:28:38.580
GCS will notice that there
is consistent load to each

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:39.450
of those prefixes.

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:40.380
It will split.

00:28:40.380 --> 00:28:43.210
And you'll be able to
double your total throughput

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:45.420
rate that you can achieve.

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:48.420
Similarly, if you don't
care about listing order,

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:51.750
you can hash the object name
and insert that hash somewhere

00:28:51.750 --> 00:28:54.300
into the object name prefix.

00:28:54.300 --> 00:28:59.570
And this will cause your load
to essentially randomly hit,

00:28:59.570 --> 00:29:02.280
essentially, what looks like
random object names, which

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:05.399
will provide a nice, even load.

00:29:05.399 --> 00:29:06.940
So again, the drawback
there, though,

00:29:06.940 --> 00:29:09.891
is that your listing order
is going to be different.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:13.060
OK.

00:29:13.060 --> 00:29:15.143
Next I'm going to talk
about timeouts and retries.

00:29:15.143 --> 00:29:17.504
And it sounds dry, but it's
really important for data

00:29:17.504 --> 00:29:19.420
processing workloads
because you are typically

00:29:19.420 --> 00:29:22.120
spiking up in your traffic.

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:25.480
And you're more likely to hit
some temporary bottlenecks.

00:29:25.480 --> 00:29:28.210
And so generally when people
think timeouts and retries,

00:29:28.210 --> 00:29:31.590
use exponential backoff and
jitter and we're done, right?

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:34.070
This is a little bit more
complicated than that.

00:29:34.070 --> 00:29:37.150
So aside from using
exponential backoff and jitter,

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:39.400
the first recommendation
we have is

00:29:39.400 --> 00:29:42.370
to use idle timeouts instead
of absolute timeouts.

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:46.450
And the reason why is imagine
the system temporarily

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:47.972
has constrained bandwidth.

00:29:47.972 --> 00:29:49.930
And you set an absolute
timeout of two seconds,

00:29:49.930 --> 00:29:51.471
but your request
needs, I don't know,

00:29:51.471 --> 00:29:53.434
let's say two and 1/2
seconds to complete.

00:29:53.434 --> 00:29:54.850
What you'll end
up doing is you'll

00:29:54.850 --> 00:29:57.580
end up waiting for two
seconds, canceling the request,

00:29:57.580 --> 00:30:00.880
retrying it, waiting two seconds
again, canceling the request,

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:04.190
and you never end
up making progress.

00:30:04.190 --> 00:30:06.610
So if you, instead,
were tracking,

00:30:06.610 --> 00:30:08.980
when was the last
time I made progress,

00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:11.170
and after two seconds
of no progress

00:30:11.170 --> 00:30:14.230
canceling your request, then you
end up handling this case much

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:16.210
more gracefully.

00:30:16.210 --> 00:30:18.220
You'll go ahead and
allow entire request

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:19.387
to take two and 1/2 seconds.

00:30:19.387 --> 00:30:21.720
And you're going to be able
to achieve positive goodput,

00:30:21.720 --> 00:30:24.550
even though throughput-- or,
sorry, available bandwidth-- is

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:26.680
decreasing.

00:30:26.680 --> 00:30:29.815
The second recommendation,
aside from exponential backoff

00:30:29.815 --> 00:30:32.830
and jitter, is to use new
connections for retries.

00:30:32.830 --> 00:30:34.690
So the reason you
wanted to do this

00:30:34.690 --> 00:30:37.294
is that if you get an
error, it's oftentimes

00:30:37.294 --> 00:30:39.460
because you happened to hit
one of the machines that

00:30:39.460 --> 00:30:41.020
aren't in our large fleet.

00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:43.492
And that machine
might be acting up.

00:30:43.492 --> 00:30:44.950
Again, we have
many, many machines.

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:47.230
And so it's possible
that any given machine is

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:50.230
unhealthy for a period of time.

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:51.760
So by establishing
a new connection

00:30:51.760 --> 00:30:54.280
you're increasing the
likelihood that you connect

00:30:54.280 --> 00:30:56.200
to a new front end machine.

00:30:56.200 --> 00:30:58.220
And you're not reissuing
the same requests

00:30:58.220 --> 00:31:00.320
to an unhealthy
front end machine.

00:31:02.960 --> 00:31:05.050
The third recommendation
is to make

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:07.060
sure to use
idempotent sequences.

00:31:07.060 --> 00:31:11.380
So whenever you are issuing
retries, if it mutates state,

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:14.440
you're at risk of having
unintended consequences.

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:17.020
And the common pattern
we end up seeing here

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:18.910
is folks who issue a delete.

00:31:18.910 --> 00:31:22.060
So imagine you issue a delete
request and that request

00:31:22.060 --> 00:31:23.140
is taking a long time.

00:31:23.140 --> 00:31:24.730
So you stop waiting.

00:31:24.730 --> 00:31:26.710
You issue it again.

00:31:26.710 --> 00:31:28.599
And the second request
succeeds quickly

00:31:28.599 --> 00:31:30.640
because you've used the
previous recommendations.

00:31:30.640 --> 00:31:32.240
It had hit a new machine.

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:35.150
And now you've gone ahead and
you've deleted your object.

00:31:35.150 --> 00:31:40.370
Now imagine you write a
new version of that object.

00:31:40.370 --> 00:31:42.380
And so you've got a
newly created version.

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:44.570
And that succeeds as well.

00:31:44.570 --> 00:31:47.260
And finally, what happens
if that very first request

00:31:47.260 --> 00:31:50.200
that was taking a long time
actually ends up succeeding?

00:31:50.200 --> 00:31:51.880
Well, that very first
request is going

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:54.414
to delete the
newly-created object.

00:31:54.414 --> 00:31:56.080
And all of a sudden,
you have data loss.

00:31:56.080 --> 00:31:59.770
So this is pretty severe if
you're not careful about it.

00:31:59.770 --> 00:32:01.540
So when we say use
idempotent sequences,

00:32:01.540 --> 00:32:03.380
there's a couple of
ways you can do this.

00:32:03.380 --> 00:32:06.760
One way is to simply
make sure you only write

00:32:06.760 --> 00:32:09.320
and never overwrite an object.

00:32:09.320 --> 00:32:11.470
And that's pretty
draconian in terms

00:32:11.470 --> 00:32:12.881
of what you're able to do.

00:32:12.881 --> 00:32:14.380
Another approach
is to use something

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:16.480
we call preconditions,
which allows

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:19.120
you to add a precondition--
it allows you to add

00:32:19.120 --> 00:32:22.480
a condition to a request that
causes the request to abort

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:23.990
if that condition isn't met.

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:26.170
So in that previous
example, I could have said,

00:32:26.170 --> 00:32:31.010
please delete object A only
if it's generation number one.

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:35.240
And if I did that, then when I
created the object A later on,

00:32:35.240 --> 00:32:36.870
it would have a
different generation,

00:32:36.870 --> 00:32:38.360
generation number two.

00:32:38.360 --> 00:32:40.700
And now that slow delete
request would no longer

00:32:40.700 --> 00:32:43.145
apply to that generation
and it would be harmless.

00:32:47.290 --> 00:32:50.220
The fourth recommendation
is to limit retry volume.

00:32:50.220 --> 00:32:54.040
And a rough rule of thumb is to
limit your retries to, perhaps,

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:55.960
10% of your total traffic.

00:32:55.960 --> 00:33:00.610
The rationale here is that if
the service is truly unhealthy,

00:33:00.610 --> 00:33:03.700
then additional retries
beyond this won't really help.

00:33:03.700 --> 00:33:06.850
And so retries are typically
for the transient sort

00:33:06.850 --> 00:33:09.940
of low error percentage cases.

00:33:09.940 --> 00:33:11.680
This helps out the
service, clearly,

00:33:11.680 --> 00:33:13.040
because you're not slamming it.

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:14.440
But it also helps your client.

00:33:14.440 --> 00:33:16.690
One of the common
things we see is clients

00:33:16.690 --> 00:33:18.670
will retry indefinitely.

00:33:18.670 --> 00:33:21.827
And they'll end up building
a queue within themselves.

00:33:21.827 --> 00:33:23.410
And it causes the
client to fall over.

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:26.950
So this ends up helping
with flow control a bit

00:33:26.950 --> 00:33:30.110
if you limit your retries.

00:33:30.110 --> 00:33:32.300
And finally, the last
recommendation we have,

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:33.710
it's a little bit of
an advanced technique,

00:33:33.710 --> 00:33:35.150
so you really
should only use this

00:33:35.150 --> 00:33:38.330
if you really need to
control your tail latency.

00:33:38.330 --> 00:33:40.490
But it's to go ahead
and use hedged requests.

00:33:40.490 --> 00:33:44.120
And a hedged request is
essentially a request

00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:48.230
that you issue if your initial
request doesn't come back

00:33:48.230 --> 00:33:49.200
in time.

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:51.644
So for example, you might
use your P95 latency--

00:33:51.644 --> 00:33:53.810
I gave a rough rule of
thumb, about 100 milliseconds

00:33:53.810 --> 00:33:54.610
for reads--

00:33:54.610 --> 00:33:56.300
and you might wait
100 milliseconds

00:33:56.300 --> 00:33:58.850
for that initial
request to come back.

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:01.520
If it doesn't, you can
issue a second request.

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:03.237
And in most cases,
if you got unlucky

00:34:03.237 --> 00:34:04.820
and that first request
happened to hit

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:06.962
just one of the rare
unhealthy machines,

00:34:06.962 --> 00:34:09.170
the second request is going
to complete very quickly,

00:34:09.170 --> 00:34:12.900
typically under 100
milliseconds 95% of the time.

00:34:12.900 --> 00:34:17.060
So this means your
99.75% latency

00:34:17.060 --> 00:34:23.300
will be under 200 milliseconds
with this technique.

00:34:23.300 --> 00:34:26.900
In the rare case where
the system is overloaded,

00:34:26.900 --> 00:34:29.239
the initial request
is still ongoing.

00:34:29.239 --> 00:34:32.100
And so you can go ahead and wait
for that to complete, as well.

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:34.280
And so this doesn't really
have too many drawbacks,

00:34:34.280 --> 00:34:38.929
as long as you are limiting
the total number of retries

00:34:38.929 --> 00:34:39.982
that you are issuing.

00:34:39.982 --> 00:34:41.690
So this is, again, an
advanced technique.

00:34:41.690 --> 00:34:42.429
If you're interested
in it, there

00:34:42.429 --> 00:34:44.719
was a great paper called
"The Tail at Scale",

00:34:44.719 --> 00:34:46.079
written by Jeff Dean.

00:34:46.079 --> 00:34:47.120
And you can look that up.

00:34:47.120 --> 00:34:49.536
And it goes into little bit
more detail on how to do this.

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:56.210
Finally, I wanted to talk a
little bit about the Hadoop use

00:34:56.210 --> 00:34:58.110
case because I
know a lot of folks

00:34:58.110 --> 00:35:00.620
have Hadoop workloads on-prem
and they're thinking about how

00:35:00.620 --> 00:35:01.760
to move it to the cloud.

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:06.520
And I wanted to mention that we
have a service called Dataproc.

00:35:06.520 --> 00:35:08.030
It's a managed Hadoop service.

00:35:08.030 --> 00:35:09.860
It allows you to
bring up new clusters.

00:35:09.860 --> 00:35:11.660
It implements a lot
of the best practices

00:35:11.660 --> 00:35:13.190
that we've talked about.

00:35:13.190 --> 00:35:18.470
In particular, it configures
HDFS with local SSD by default.

00:35:18.470 --> 00:35:20.690
So you can use that
for temporary data

00:35:20.690 --> 00:35:22.830
or intermediate data.

00:35:22.830 --> 00:35:26.840
It also installs a Hadoop
file system connector for GCS

00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:28.140
by default, as well.

00:35:28.140 --> 00:35:31.070
So what this allows you to do
is it allows your Hadoop jobs

00:35:31.070 --> 00:35:34.670
to access source data directly
from GCS without first copying

00:35:34.670 --> 00:35:36.310
it into your HDFS cluster.

00:35:38.992 --> 00:35:40.700
A lot of the best
practices we've already

00:35:40.700 --> 00:35:44.430
talked about-- using local SSD,
paralyzing small operations.

00:35:44.430 --> 00:35:46.430
The other thing I
wanted to mention,

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:48.500
the biggest difference
I think folks

00:35:48.500 --> 00:35:52.670
find when they move from
on-prem Hadoop to the cloud,

00:35:52.670 --> 00:35:56.540
is that Cloud Storage
is a object storage, not

00:35:56.540 --> 00:35:57.830
a file system.

00:35:57.830 --> 00:36:00.530
So it doesn't have a
true directory semantics.

00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:03.140
For example, it doesn't support
atomic directory rename.

00:36:03.140 --> 00:36:04.640
So this will
oftentimes require you

00:36:04.640 --> 00:36:07.560
to tweak your job a little bit.

00:36:07.560 --> 00:36:09.770
For example, one common
pattern we've seen

00:36:09.770 --> 00:36:13.220
is folks will write files
into a temporary directory

00:36:13.220 --> 00:36:15.380
and then when that temporary
directory is complete,

00:36:15.380 --> 00:36:17.046
they'll atomically
rename that directory

00:36:17.046 --> 00:36:18.290
into a final location.

00:36:18.290 --> 00:36:20.640
And that signals a later part
of their pipeline to say,

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:22.457
hey, all my data is here.

00:36:22.457 --> 00:36:25.040
So because Cloud Storage doesn't
support that atomic directory

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:27.380
operation, an
alternative method is

00:36:27.380 --> 00:36:30.360
to write your objects into
the final destination.

00:36:30.360 --> 00:36:32.600
And when all of your
objects are complete,

00:36:32.600 --> 00:36:34.430
you can write a success file.

00:36:34.430 --> 00:36:36.020
And the presence of
that success file

00:36:36.020 --> 00:36:39.344
says, hey, the next stage of
the pipeline is ready to go.

00:36:39.344 --> 00:36:40.760
So there are some
tricks with this

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:43.640
that you might need to do
to update your pipeline

00:36:43.640 --> 00:36:49.550
to get it to work around the
lack of directory atomicity.

00:36:49.550 --> 00:36:51.800
OK, with that, I'm going to
hand it back over to Geoff

00:36:51.800 --> 00:36:53.174
to talk about
backup and archive.

00:36:56.225 --> 00:36:57.100
GEOFFREY NOER: Great.

00:36:57.100 --> 00:36:59.390
So backup and archive
is the last area

00:36:59.390 --> 00:37:01.880
that we'll talk about today.

00:37:01.880 --> 00:37:04.100
And a little bit
shorter because this

00:37:04.100 --> 00:37:07.760
is a relatively simple
but important area.

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:13.690
And we have two different
cool storage classes.

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:17.680
So we talk about the different
hotness and coolness of data.

00:37:17.680 --> 00:37:21.780
And the main multi-regional
class and regional classes,

00:37:21.780 --> 00:37:24.540
those are our hot tier.

00:37:24.540 --> 00:37:29.340
But if you want to tier down
to less frequently-accessed

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:34.075
storage, this is where Nearline
and Coldline come into play.

00:37:34.075 --> 00:37:38.100
And here, the difference,
all of the storage classes

00:37:38.100 --> 00:37:39.390
are instant access speeds.

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:43.170
So you're never waiting for
data to come off of tape.

00:37:43.170 --> 00:37:45.190
The latencies are very
similar across all

00:37:45.190 --> 00:37:47.280
of the storage classes.

00:37:47.280 --> 00:37:49.030
You're always using
the same APIs.

00:37:49.030 --> 00:37:51.240
So that's also consistent.

00:37:51.240 --> 00:37:53.400
And you always benefit
from strong consistency.

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:55.740
So actually, there's
more commonality

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:57.390
across the different
storage classes

00:37:57.390 --> 00:37:59.920
than there are differences.

00:37:59.920 --> 00:38:02.040
But those differences
come down to what

00:38:02.040 --> 00:38:04.530
is the ideal access frequency.

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:07.960
And for Nearline, you'll
find that, of course,

00:38:07.960 --> 00:38:09.780
you can access more
frequently than this.

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:12.990
But if you limit it to no
more than once per month,

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:15.720
then your pricing will end up
being really advantageous to be

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:18.270
in the Nearline storage class.

00:38:18.270 --> 00:38:22.740
And similarly, if you expect
to access less often than once

00:38:22.740 --> 00:38:26.160
per year for truly
cold-archived data,

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:31.060
then you'll benefit strongly
from using a Coldline class.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:35.070
So the way that this is priced
is basically the hot tiers,

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:38.204
you pay more per gigabyte month.

00:38:38.204 --> 00:38:40.620
But on the flip side, there's
actually no retrieval charge

00:38:40.620 --> 00:38:41.910
in the hot classes.

00:38:41.910 --> 00:38:43.800
For Nearline and
Coldline, you start

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:48.640
to pay more for retrieval as
your storage class goes down.

00:38:48.640 --> 00:38:52.870
But the performance
behavior is very consistent.

00:38:52.870 --> 00:38:55.590
So the use cases for
Nearline and Coldline,

00:38:55.590 --> 00:38:59.130
because of that frequency
of access difference,

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:02.640
you tend to see backups
actually being more associated

00:39:02.640 --> 00:39:04.890
with Nearline than Coldline.

00:39:04.890 --> 00:39:07.050
Anything around
content serving where

00:39:07.050 --> 00:39:10.600
it's a long-tail archive that's
just infrequently accessed

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:14.040
but it needs to be available
and ready to share,

00:39:14.040 --> 00:39:18.060
that would be usually more
of a Nearline access pattern.

00:39:18.060 --> 00:39:20.580
And then some
analytics workloads

00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:22.710
where the data is very
infrequently accessed,

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:24.300
you're better off with Nearline.

00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:28.800
Coldline is really much more
about the long-term archives--

00:39:28.800 --> 00:39:32.460
so tape replacements, disaster
recovery implementations,

00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:34.989
multimedia source
file storage where

00:39:34.989 --> 00:39:37.530
you need to have it around but
you're really not accessing it

00:39:37.530 --> 00:39:43.140
very frequently, those are
all cases to use Coldline.

00:39:43.140 --> 00:39:47.760
Now, these storage classes
don't exist just on their own.

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:51.330
It's really part of a larger
storage tiering capability

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:53.540
that we offer.

00:39:53.540 --> 00:39:55.860
So you can set up an
automated lifecycle

00:39:55.860 --> 00:39:57.840
which will move data
automatically based

00:39:57.840 --> 00:39:59.500
on the age of the objects.

00:39:59.500 --> 00:40:01.710
So this is just one
particular illustration

00:40:01.710 --> 00:40:05.280
here where you've
chosen, in this example,

00:40:05.280 --> 00:40:08.640
to say, after 30 days,
move from the hot tiers

00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:10.920
of either multi-regional
or regional

00:40:10.920 --> 00:40:12.480
and move those to Nearline.

00:40:12.480 --> 00:40:14.700
And then after 90 days
of additional storage

00:40:14.700 --> 00:40:17.100
there, move it to Coldline,
and then after a year,

00:40:17.100 --> 00:40:18.450
delete the data.

00:40:18.450 --> 00:40:22.145
So maybe that's what
your profile looks like.

00:40:22.145 --> 00:40:23.520
If it's not, you
could tweak this

00:40:23.520 --> 00:40:26.460
to however you want it to be.

00:40:26.460 --> 00:40:28.500
But what's nice
is you can really

00:40:28.500 --> 00:40:32.040
set it up so that the objects
are migrated automatically

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:38.090
down to lower cost storage as
the age of the data increases.

00:40:41.010 --> 00:40:45.360
So I mean, number one thing
to say about backup is, just

00:40:45.360 --> 00:40:49.350
because it's cloud doesn't
make it automagically

00:40:49.350 --> 00:40:52.800
protected against every
single possible incidence

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:54.810
and occurrence that might occur.

00:40:54.810 --> 00:40:59.400
Just like in an on-prem, there
are still backup considerations

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:01.560
that you need to take
into account for Cloud.

00:41:01.560 --> 00:41:05.070
So don't overlook having a
backup strategy, number one.

00:41:05.070 --> 00:41:07.100
We provide a lot
of features that

00:41:07.100 --> 00:41:10.840
aid you in preventing
accidental deletion.

00:41:10.840 --> 00:41:13.350
So a few of those that
are the most important

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:18.780
include object versioning,
the lifecycle capabilities,

00:41:18.780 --> 00:41:21.550
and being able to lock down
delete support for backups.

00:41:21.550 --> 00:41:25.470
So all of those help isolate
you against accidental deletion

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:26.710
of data.

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:30.810
And especially for the
backups of on-prem data,

00:41:30.810 --> 00:41:36.190
so cloud backup capabilities,
the general rules

00:41:36.190 --> 00:41:39.030
of understanding what
your backup windows are

00:41:39.030 --> 00:41:42.000
and what your restore
times look like are

00:41:42.000 --> 00:41:46.770
equally applicable to Cloud as
they are for on-prem backup.

00:41:46.770 --> 00:41:50.820
It's the latencies and the
size of the network pipes

00:41:50.820 --> 00:41:53.420
that are in place
between your data center

00:41:53.420 --> 00:41:55.459
and Cloud that count.

00:41:55.459 --> 00:41:57.000
So understanding
what your throughput

00:41:57.000 --> 00:41:59.010
is, how fast can you
do those backups,

00:41:59.010 --> 00:42:01.800
how fast can you
restore, that helps you.

00:42:01.800 --> 00:42:04.680
As long as you can set that up
to meet your business needs,

00:42:04.680 --> 00:42:08.010
you'll be set with Cloud Backup.

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:10.620
And we do see different
deployment patterns for backup.

00:42:10.620 --> 00:42:11.910
We have the Cloud backup.

00:42:11.910 --> 00:42:14.910
We also have Cloud
gateway type setups

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:17.820
where there's maybe a
dedupe engine in the middle.

00:42:17.820 --> 00:42:20.040
There are a lot of different
ways to set this up.

00:42:20.040 --> 00:42:22.020
I won't spend too
much time on this

00:42:22.020 --> 00:42:26.880
except to really emphasize
that one area that we've really

00:42:26.880 --> 00:42:32.040
emphasized with our partners
is the native integrations

00:42:32.040 --> 00:42:35.200
of Cloud Storage with
our backup partners.

00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:38.010
So these are native
integrations taking advantage

00:42:38.010 --> 00:42:40.740
of all the features of both
our backup partners and Cloud

00:42:40.740 --> 00:42:41.760
Storage.

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:45.570
And they include partnerships
with Commvault, Veritas,

00:42:45.570 --> 00:42:50.190
Cohesity, Actifio, Rubrik,
Dell EMC, Unitrends, CloudBerry

00:42:50.190 --> 00:42:51.460
Labs, others.

00:42:51.460 --> 00:42:55.080
So this is a very comprehensive
portfolio of partners.

00:42:55.080 --> 00:42:56.640
And we encourage
you to check out

00:42:56.640 --> 00:42:58.680
those integrations
for your backup needs

00:42:58.680 --> 00:43:02.520
because it really simplifies
taking advantages of those best

00:43:02.520 --> 00:43:06.580
practices and just making
it happen smoothly.

00:43:06.580 --> 00:43:11.110
So with that, I'd like to thank
you all for attending today.

00:43:11.110 --> 00:43:12.430
So thank you very much.

00:43:12.430 --> 00:43:12.930
[APPLAUSE]

00:43:12.930 --> 00:43:16.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

